[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179573 :question/id #uuid "5ebfa0d1-bf16-56d0-a58f-2b16720b7aad" :question/math-academy-id "e-9439" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fda8bfb0533b86dc4050d1b8e309e332996b26263b1052d3e251bf0a5c98e816.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C}12xy\\:\\textrm{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter-ellipse $4x^2+y^2=4$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-1,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{y^2}{2^2}  = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3{cos}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = 12xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =12\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}12xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{1+3{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =24{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+3\\cos^2 {t},$ $\\textrm d u=-6\\sin t\\cos \n t\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-4{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}({4}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}(8-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{56}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179578 :question/id #uuid "1990e97c-7244-5b6d-a5d5-69cd4b91ad21" :question/math-academy-id "e-9460" :question/problem "What expression is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_\\limits{C} \\dfrac{x}{y} \\: \\textrm{d}s,$ where $C$ is the ellipse $x^2 + 16y^2 = 16,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4^2} + \\dfrac{y^2}{1^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 4\\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t} \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=4\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179579 :question/id #uuid "a4c10c26-84c5-5ec7-9468-93986e27f492" :question/math-academy-id "e-9462" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y) =x^2-2$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 4.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2-2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t-2 \\\\  & =2(2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t,$ $\\textrm d u=2\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{0}^{4\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{4\\pi } \\\\  & =2(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179580 :question/id #uuid "f025deb6-7c71-5d15-9966-52f7d5eeab80" :question/math-academy-id "e-9463" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0cebdd804ff244d037e14df6ec33efb86b522644f7b4addc688e27fd6a57e95.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} y^3e^{-x} \\: \\textrm{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x = \\ln{y}$ from $(0, 1)$ to $\\left(\\dfrac32\\ln 2 , 2\\sqrt{2}\\right),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = x(t) = \\ln{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle \\ln{t}, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = y^3e^{-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3}{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{3}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u = t^2 + 1,$ $\\textrm{d}u = 2t \\, \\textrm{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{2}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{9}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{27-2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179586 :question/id #uuid "b7d08596-fbe4-5601-a58e-dac90715f7e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9486" :question/problem "Find a definite integral that is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = x+\\sin{y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+t[(i+j)-i] \\\\  & =i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1$ and $y=t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =0\\,i+j \\\\  & =j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x+\\sin{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179587 :question/id #uuid "17ec17a0-11d7-57c1-9e7f-6893ab9e51e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9487" :question/problem "Find the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y-z,$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-2\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(j-3k)+t[(-\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k)-(j-3k)] \\\\  & =(j-3k)+t(-i+\\,j+k) \\\\  & =-t\\,i+(t+1)\\,j+(t-3)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-t, y=t+1,$ and $z=t-3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-3)k \\\\  & =-i+j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2+y-z$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(-t)^{2}+(t+1)-(t-3) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179588 :question/id #uuid "239120b8-0553-5cff-aee1-f50253af11ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-9488" :question/problem "![](images/71/71df4fc7bd52fe5dd97c7e01502dec2ad68ae31fdf224cccd812c6b7318c70b7.png)\n\nFind, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C} 2xy \\:\\textrm{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the right half of the ellipse $9x^2 + 4y^2 = 36,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{2^2} + \\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 3\\sin{t} \\right\\rangle, \\qquad -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = 2xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179608 :question/id #uuid "337d9d7e-5488-5e6d-af34-e01d22d3d0b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9574" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6eebbc5ae9f548f5b00de9a12d454fa788884bf73ad46a1065eab0d26330a921.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y = \\dfrac14x^2$ from the point $\\left(0,0\\right)$ to the point $\\left(4,4\\right).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad\ny = \\dfrac{t^2}{4},\n\\qquad\nt\\in \\left[0,4\\right].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(4,4).$ Therefore, $\\mathbf n$ is indeed a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/2b/2b94e24344f600fc02ec5ecac6c1e0ee4c6a5450132753a0a5ac5fceaa9aa1fc.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-2, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})=\\frac{t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (\\frac{t}{2})-(t)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}-2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-16-0 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179609 :question/id #uuid "5f6de8db-7738-5df7-8cf8-8e37f6ab483f" :question/math-academy-id "e-96" :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0} \\:\\dfrac{\\tan 2\\theta}{\\theta}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\tan 2θ}{θ} &= lim_(θ → 0)((\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{\\cos 2θ}))/(θ) \\\\\n&= lim_(θ → 0)(\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ}) \\\\\n&= lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$. So, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n$\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin 2\\theta}{\\theta} = \\dfrac{0}{0}$,\nwhich is an indeterminate form. Instead, let's recall the following special limit:\n$\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x} =1$We rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n$lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ}|= lim_(θ → 0)(2 \\cdot \\frac{\\sin 2θ}{2θ}); = 2 \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin2θ}{2θ}$Let $x = 2\\theta$. Then, since $x\\to0$ as $\\theta \\to 0$, we have\n$2 \\cdot \\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin \\color{blue}2\\theta}{\\color{blue}2\\theta} = 2 \\cdot \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin \\color{blue}x}{\\color{blue}x} = 2 \\cdot 1 =2$.\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n$\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0} \\: \\dfrac{1}{\\cos 2\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos 0} = \\dfrac11 = 1$.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nlim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ} = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "112fa180-a9ff-5a56-b2fe-80282d87c197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253821} {:answer/id #uuid "60cf1536-90f0-50e5-951e-3b49ab45ab85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254535} {:answer/id #uuid "bc1a3b6b-ca8b-5902-8ca8-8bc8bf6be7a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255352} {:answer/id #uuid "e0be5a81-a209-5ccd-96ce-0c6a965890c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255666} {:answer/id #uuid "e86b4ea1-a0c8-561f-af0b-f93a91a9e5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60cf1536-90f0-50e5-951e-3b49ab45ab85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254535} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f0a4ffd-5d50-5cdb-9c19-928ff9fa4767" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b79282b0-117d-50c1-87eb-8d6109e91a7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122136" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =16{cos}^{2}⁡t-16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =16({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t,$ $\\text{d}u=2\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =32{\\int }_{0}^{4\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =32\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{4\\pi } \\\\  & =32(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c24c262-4398-5446-bc9d-27645b4f0cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}(2\\cos t + \\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177424} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c38c61-4b53-5b3b-9117-947ce6775169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}(\\cos t + 2\\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177425} {:answer/id #uuid "de07f689-6383-5efe-90dd-91b2b4623e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}(\\cos t + 2\\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177426} {:answer/id #uuid "0eaf656d-f304-5384-9efa-03f1f9909af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}(2\\cos t + \\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177427} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce387ae-f4c2-5ae9-a54a-6936b3b4fe44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}(\\cos^{2} t + 2\\sin t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177428}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8f90318c-819f-5544-b17a-bb82ed972697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177423} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "493c72c7-d782-5717-bb31-6b0270646703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387176} {:answer/id #uuid "a9663772-7b34-5824-82d4-530f49fc606a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387177} {:answer/id #uuid "c397de39-3c94-523e-b199-d82ded805b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387178} {:answer/id #uuid "9e22557f-8b13-5a8f-9cf2-c6c3704ac0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387179} {:answer/id #uuid "dad10713-d816-5d20-9f77-54c2d84897ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dad10713-d816-5d20-9f77-54c2d84897ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387180} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7ee2bd2-6018-5d27-a3ae-041cc035912b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8f5c163-ed26-54b5-8aab-82f3a498034d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122263" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18621f5a-7133-571b-88ef-53eb0deed3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253880} {:answer/id #uuid "644bb80a-08b0-5a24-bfe8-6878fa59640a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{5{sin}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254569} {:answer/id #uuid "9c6a3057-6ace-5530-947c-d9f8b98ee510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+15}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255048} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e161d4-6bd1-5a00-a1f3-e07b0c70af01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{5{sin}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255171} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1ba099-2805-5efa-92dd-2e4223b1218b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18621f5a-7133-571b-88ef-53eb0deed3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253880} :answer-field/id #uuid "56c08944-18ef-5fdc-9330-4e14892bd61a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "939eabb6-2bce-5cdb-8479-d0b9ae63294a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122269" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3bf746cfb21805587a65c7beeac90e75464f630bab1fbf3453de69ca3e6639eb.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{4y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the left half of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=16,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{4y}{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{4y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "814fc6cf-8415-51dc-aa71-2b2dcbf81962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{53}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254814} {:answer/id #uuid "86f05304-f4c1-54b6-a4c5-000eafd2bbb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{47}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254865} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b6b72a-eb09-5d1b-85c0-269d51d0e83c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{67}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255324} {:answer/id #uuid "be619077-ef60-58f6-93be-483e6d9cbf83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{51}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255369} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf2dc83-c0d2-5f55-bd25-8b17407e5e55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccf2dc83-c0d2-5f55-bd25-8b17407e5e55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255492} :answer-field/id #uuid "50ce09ca-94dc-54bf-a873-caeb486ff0fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a72d2a3-84b0-5b95-afa8-d5133b8bf8f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-122271" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola ${y}^{2}=8x$ from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{9}{8},3).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=8x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{4}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{t}{4})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{16}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{24x}{y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{24x}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\cdot (\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8})}{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3t\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+16,$ $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{16}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{16}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({25}^{3/2}-{16}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(125-64) \\\\  & =\\frac{61}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e7f000-2d2b-551e-80dd-e04e2acee2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253701} {:answer/id #uuid "1dfb99c9-c4f2-5b3f-bd12-1c6c7092dd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253922} {:answer/id #uuid "4821693d-3621-5434-a86b-7624d2be2de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254283} {:answer/id #uuid "8efd5e79-5f84-56f4-a89d-cbfaa679144d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254926} {:answer/id #uuid "f844b804-93be-5b90-bda6-d59e3a5be4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dfb99c9-c4f2-5b3f-bd12-1c6c7092dd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253922} :answer-field/id #uuid "72ee3597-1dd0-5de1-b56b-43f4da9ab07f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dcd58e0c-8630-5c7a-a6a1-6bb6a8bcb260" :question/math-academy-id "q-122440" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(4,0)$ in the fourth quadrant?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](images/0b/0baaafbe9846ccef2c06a3b1a86eccac394344afc2dfd94bba2907ebb1cf2362.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba90d78-1161-52a4-93c0-d995aaaf7e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6c/6cf967e8a5d3bc7b2ac021414875f5221ef52790dfbdd6e214cccc7f0e8be2d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387299} {:answer/id #uuid "bb982230-23d8-541a-a311-793fcd335e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4b/4be9586aaa886ccfe092f93c106a5f7233509c798c9a90ecfda0363173dc7afb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387300} {:answer/id #uuid "6f167c07-25dc-5f9e-956f-2fe0933e7ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/95/9593ba00b964194c1d91549fe38a1334a478ffd6b724534c4512a11d19d683aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387301} {:answer/id #uuid "640a700e-3b7c-59e8-83e6-b88bdc953d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f8/f814f8c7e6d879cbe25e5108704e29464d0c39859371ee4fac3bfb3e72f90696.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387302} {:answer/id #uuid "09b9b72e-d802-5315-b38d-ee456018cf2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/26/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09b9b72e-d802-5315-b38d-ee456018cf2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/26/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387303} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7fe3fc1-2c7d-5b68-96b7-12857eae01aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe3ebbef-9b0f-50cb-ab18-6df5f7b1d83c" :question/math-academy-id "q-122789" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}\\,i-y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2),$ $(2,-3),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,2) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (-2)^{2}\\,i-2\\,j=3\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-3) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-(-3)\\,j=3\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-\\,j=3\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/26/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c16fe8e-df28-5acf-80d4-30daf328aacf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253906} {:answer/id #uuid "5aca3d77-fbac-59a8-a8e8-f141fffb748d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254472} {:answer/id #uuid "6641d652-1c79-5594-8a40-67438b550538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254589} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a3d234-e957-5e8d-85be-bdebd0026797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255242} {:answer/id #uuid "edb11fd3-821e-5d36-891b-095d0de53681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6641d652-1c79-5594-8a40-67438b550538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254589} :answer-field/id #uuid "761e5ab1-caf4-5260-859c-054aab437559" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09a46495-b2d7-513a-938f-504451202710" :question/math-academy-id "q-123127" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,2)$ to $(3,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,2)$ to $(3,1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=2\\,j$ and $b=3\\,i+j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =2\\,j+t[(3\\,i+j)-2\\,j] \\\\  & =2\\,j+t(3\\,i-j) \\\\  & =3t\\,i+(2-t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3t$ and $y=2-t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i-j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t)^{2}-(2-t) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22a10aec-2de8-522a-80d0-b6ac69c3fc36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+5}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257069} {:answer/id #uuid "245e377f-d794-5faa-80dc-046dd074c5d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257088} {:answer/id #uuid "59416cb9-04ed-59ee-bb6f-e3a4d50a609c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257556} {:answer/id #uuid "922d00d6-8ac5-59b8-b6b0-c621f39534d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258071} {:answer/id #uuid "aa428e1b-e786-5467-88b9-85fb757023e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+5}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59416cb9-04ed-59ee-bb6f-e3a4d50a609c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257556} :answer-field/id #uuid "f90be87a-aed6-507f-bb3d-7e829ec213e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f83a1e2-579b-5a18-9def-ff09a4759ecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-123193" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b0f3ae9a464812957c297c09ff3f65bfe5964bdac70298e72ec3dbde2325e72.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}(6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the right half of the ellipse $6{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=6,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $\\sqrt{6},$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =6{cos}^{2}⁡t-6{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =6({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "711177e4-868e-5ac6-a2b1-961ab8f94f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257775} {:answer/id #uuid "8860493e-bab1-52a0-9bf9-eb7d11745d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257977} {:answer/id #uuid "b78bc20f-293d-5acf-9926-a6d8bb185d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258386} {:answer/id #uuid "db0fb6b5-5dcc-57a4-a8f1-684f9c0bb985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258695} {:answer/id #uuid "f570bf13-b1d2-5f54-853f-bb075d272c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db0fb6b5-5dcc-57a4-a8f1-684f9c0bb985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258695} :answer-field/id #uuid "d19b03b6-7a81-55e6-a56b-0d4882ea6475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fb44c65-3cac-5280-b910-d92f6181f8ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-123269" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(0,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(0,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t[3\\,j-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)-t\\,i \\\\  & =(1-t)\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1-t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)j \\\\  & =-i \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-t)+3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180044 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82419925-9208-5fb7-aeb8-1c2b460f3d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177934} {:answer/id #uuid "ae358084-2a52-5121-a957-ccc00fc2b986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177935} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e72b9c-5d7c-5fc7-8742-f34b5d473a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177936} {:answer/id #uuid "02f393d9-a672-5d17-9a29-a1440878bd81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177937} {:answer/id #uuid "5b31f798-8fdd-59fb-8d05-4698e80b7f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177938}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3893be01-8223-5deb-81ac-945c190891e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177933}] :question/id #uuid "01c89c10-f3d0-5af6-a0bd-1594a28b8902" :question/math-academy-id "q-127981" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\frac{1}{8} \\div (-\\frac{1}{24})$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180045 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96f807c8-4c92-59d2-832e-9a8799cb416f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177940} {:answer/id #uuid "216e665e-c853-524e-bfcb-53cf05ed91c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177941} {:answer/id #uuid "45329cd5-f9c8-53ea-a696-3aaf0effb65e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177942} {:answer/id #uuid "4a829686-6b6d-50f8-a716-74a2521f4265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/val